# Laravel get class name of related model

In a typical Laravel application, you often need to inspect the structure of your relationships without hitting the database. Consider a scenario where you have an `Faq` model that belongs to many `Product` models. You define the relationship as follows:

```php
class Faq extends Eloquent { 
    public function products(){
        return $this->belongsToMany('Product');
    }
}
```

When working in a controller, you might want to retrieve the class name of the related modelâ`Product` in this caseâwithout executing a database query. The current approach you are using involves accessing the relationship and then fetching data:

```php
$faq = new Faq();
$className = get_class($faq->products()->get()->first());
```

This works, but it forces Laravel to execute a query just to retrieve an empty collection or a single null record. While this might seem negligible in isolation, avoiding unnecessary database hits is a core principle of efficient application design, especially when building admin panels, generating API responses, or running bulk operations.

The good news is that you can access the relationship definition itself and extract the related model's class name without querying the database. In Laravel's Eloquent ORM, relationships are stored as properties on the model instance after they are resolved. However, simply calling `$faq->products` before invoking any query methods won't automatically resolve the relationship; it returns a relationship instance, not the related models.

To get the class name of the related model without querying, you need to access the underlying foreign key or pivot table configuration defined in the relationship method. Since `belongsToMany` is used here, the relationship stores the related model's class name internally. You can leverage the `getRelated()` method provided by Eloquent relationships. This method returns the actual model class string associated with the relationship definition.

Here is how you can implement this:

```php
$faq = new Faq();
$relatedClassName = $faq->products()->getRelated();
// $relatedClassName will be "Product" (or fully qualified namespace if configured)
```

This approach is clean, efficient, and adheres to the principle of separation between schema inspection and data retrieval. The `getRelated()` method returns the string class name defined in the relationship definition, which in your case is `'Product'`. If you are using full namespaces, it will return the fully qualified class name depending on your configuration.

It is important to note that this works because the relationship instance retains metadata about the related model even before any data is fetched. This is distinct from accessing the actual related records, which would trigger a query via `get()` or `first()`. By using `getRelated()`, you are purely inspecting the definition of the relationship.

One common pitfall to avoid is assuming that all relationships behave identically regarding metadata access. While `getRelated()` works for standard associations like `hasMany`, `belongsTo`, and `belongsToMany`, always ensure that the relationship method is accessible on the model instance you are inspecting. Additionally, if you are using custom pivot tables or additional parameters in your relationship definition, these do not affect the return value of `getRelated()`, as it only reflects the primary related model class.

If you need more granular control over relationship inspection, such as checking for optional relationships or accessing foreign key column names, Laravel's relationship instances provide a robust API for introspection without requiring database interaction. This capability is particularly useful when dynamically generating forms, validating input schemas, or building flexible APIs that adapt to your application's structure on the fly.

